{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "In previous studies, food insecurity has been hypothesised to promote the prevalence of metabolic risk factors on the causal pathway to diet-sensitive non-communicable diseases (NCDs). This systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to determine the associations between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors on the causal pathway to diet-sensitive NCDs and estimate the prevalence of key metabolic risk factors among the food-insecure patients in sub-Saharan Africa.",
      "reason": "The manuscript states the current understanding (hypothesised link between food insecurity and metabolic risk factors) and frames the specific research aims, describing the state of knowledge and motivating investigation of uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Metabolic risk factors warrant particular public health effort, due to their substantial contribution to global disease burden, rapidly increasing trends, and variable patterns in incidence by age, gender, or region across ­ countries. While there are progressively better estimates of the prevalence of metabolic risk factors and proximate drivers of this prevalence in high-income countries; reliable data on the trend and patterns of key metabolic risk factors among the food insecure population in resource-limited settings, such as sub-Saharan African countries still lag far ­ behind.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the public health importance and the need for the review, noting gaps in reliable data in sub-Saharan Africa and justification for conducting the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Previous systematic reviews and several cross-sectional studies have contributed to the evidence that food insecurity is associated with metabolic risk factors on the causal pathway to diet-sensitive NCDs. ... Similar to our systematic review, Arenas et al. found an adverse association between exposure to food insecurity and dyslipidemia... A recent review by Beltran et al. showed that most studies in the USA that indicate a correlation between food insecurity and hypertension correspond to self-reporting of conditions.",
      "reason": "The manuscript cites and discusses prior reviews and studies, comparing findings and explaining how the current review relates to and extends previous work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This review is observational/epidemiological (assessing associations between exposure and outcomes), not a review of intervention effects, so a mechanism of action for an intervention is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not assess complex interventions or delivery contexts and does not present or require a logic model; thus this element does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The main research question: What are the associations between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors for diet-sensitive NCDs in the sub-Saharan African population?",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the main research question and uses the PEO framework (Population, Exposure, Outcomes) to formulate the review question."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The present systematic review and meta-analysis were conducted following the principles recommended in the Centre for Reviews and Dissemination (CRD) guidance for undertaking systematic reviews in ­ healthcare ... by systematically reviewing current peer-reviewed epidemiological studies conducted in subSaharan Africa, assessing the associations between food insecurity and key metabolic risk factors on the causal pathway to diet-sensitive non-communicable diseases (NCDs).",
      "reason": "This review examines associations from epidemiological (observational) studies rather than intervention effects; therefore PICO-style planned comparisons for interventions are not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 95
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "table_2",
      "quote": "Table 2. Eligibility criteria according to the PEO-T nomenclature criteria.\n\n| Criteria     | Inclusions                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Exclusions                                                                                                                                 |\n| Study design | Only original and quantitative study design that are peer-reviewed cross- sectional studies conducted in sub-Saharan Africa by trained personnel, in any publication language                                                                                                                                           | Qualitative study design, and systematic reviews, literature reviews, mini-reviews and cross-sectional studies conducted outside of Africa |\n| Population   | Studies conducted among sub-Saharan African populations Participants of any age group and sex that did not mix the age groups for analysis Any ethnicity, culture, or race                                                                                                                                              | Research studies conducted among people outside of sub-Saharan Africa Participants with coexisting medical conditions                      |\n| Exposure     | Studies reporting a measure of food insecurity or food insufficiency as an inde- pendent variable A measure of food insecurity beyond receiving government-sponsored nutri- tional benefits/ or living in an area designated as a food desert Food insecurity evaluated at the household level or individual level      | Research studies reporting on voluntary food restriction, as in cases of anorexia/ Nervosa                                                 |\n| Outcomes     | Studies reporting at least one of the key metabolic risk factors as a dependent variable Diagnosed with key metabolic risk factors per any of the international diagnostic criteria for MetS: NCEP-ATP III, IDF, or AHA/NHLBI criteria or any other measures in line with the World Health Organization criteria (1998) | Research studies reporting on the outcome as the result of infectious agents None validated outcome measures                               |\n| Time         | Complete peer-reviewed original research studies, published between January 2015 and June 2019                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Studies published before January 2015                                                                                                      |",
      "reason": "Table 2 explicitly lists study characteristics used to determine eligibility: study design, population, exposure, outcomes, and time frame (PEO-T framework)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "No language restrictions were imposed to minimise the risk that eligible studies could be inadvertently excluded. However, the year of publication was limited to between January 2015 and October 2019 to ensure that the evidence of associations is demonstrably rooted in the most recent and up-to-date scientific literature.",
      "reason": "The Methods state explicit eligibility restrictions for report characteristics: a publication year range and that no language restrictions were applied; Table 2 also specifies peer-reviewed original studies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No explicit statement was found distinguishing exclusion because outcomes were not measured from exclusion because outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "we recognised the need to stratify the evidence of associations as reported by the studies into three strata of associations: Adverse Association, Nonsignificant Association, and Inconclusive Association. [-] Adverse Association-Food insecurity was reported to be significantly associated with ≥ one key metabolic risk factor ... The included studies were manually coded into strata' as explained above, and presented in the compiled summary of the extracted data table (see Table 3). ... A similar type of metabolic risk factors were pooled from each study for overall estimates.",
      "reason": "The Methods describe how studies were grouped for syntheses: studies were coded into three strata of association and similar metabolic risk factors were pooled for meta-analysis, linking grouping to the review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "However, the year of publication was limited to between January 2015 and October 2019 to ensure that the evidence of associations is demonstrably rooted in the most recent and up-to-date scientific literature. This decision was chiefly because practitioners and decision-makers are somewhat interested and encouraged to make use of the most current evidence to inform policy ­ debates 26 .",
      "reason": "A rationale is provided for the notable restriction on publication year (to include recent evidence). Additionally, the Discussion notes rationale for excluding qualitative studies because the research question was epidemiological in design."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

